// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: types, integers, literals, ranges
// @expected-error: E410
// @description: An integer literal must fit the sized type it is
//   checked against.
//
//     let x: Int8 = 300;   // was accepted, then truncated
//
// THE ANSWER WAS ALREADY COMPUTED. `synth_and_check` asks
// `integer_type_range(expected)` and compares the literal against it —
// and used the result only to take a fast path. When the literal did NOT
// fit, the code fell through and the value was accepted anyway, so the
// program ran and printed whatever the truncation produced.
//
// What made it invisible is that the TYPE is checked on the same line:
// `let x: Int8 = "hello"` is refused with E400. Only the literal's RANGE
// went unasked — and `check_literal_fits` in `integer_hierarchy`, the
// function written for exactly this, has no caller in the compiler at
// all.
//
// WHY THIS IS A SPEC AND NOT A UNIT TEST: a bare `TypeChecker` does not
// know `Int8`, `Byte` or `UInt32` — the sized integer types arrive with
// the core metadata — so a unit test naming them measures "type not
// found: Int8" and passes for the wrong reason. `Int` is the only width
// that harness can speak about, and `let x: Int = <literal>` never
// reaches the coercion path at all, because the literal's own type
// already IS `Int`. The rule lives on the path where the expected type
// differs from the literal's default, which is exactly what a sized
// annotation is.

public fn too_big() -> Int8 {
    // ERROR: `Int8` holds -128 to 127.
    let x: Int8 = 300;
    x
}

fn main() {
    print(f"{too_big()}");
}
